<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nankana Sahib</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rai-Bhoi-Di-Talwandi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nankana Sahib is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, about 91 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and the headquarters of its district. It is named for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>, who was born and lived there, and its birthplace gurdwara is among the holiest Sikh sites. A town called Rai-Bhoi-Di-Talwandi was founded there under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> by Rai Bhoi, and his great-grandson Rai Bular Bhatti renamed it after the birth of Guru Nanak. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a> renovated the main gurdwara in 1819–20. On 20 February 1921 the Udasi mahant ordered his men to fire on Akali protesters, an event called the Nankana massacre. The city had 130,041 people in 2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nankana-sahib.jpg" alt="Nankana Sahib" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shaguftakarim · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Entrance_of_Janam_Asthan-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founder, Guru Nanak, was born in 1469 into a Hindu family of the Khatri caste at Talwandi, now <b>Nankana Sahib</b> in Pakistan, in the Punjab, a land where Hindu and Muslim traditions met.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
